From 628ae7dba5f3215174f71979c29e3228f8630bf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DatCaptainHorse <kristian@nestri.io>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 19:04:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] cgroup/dmem: respect protection when the common ancestor is
 the root

ttm_bo_evict_cb() passes the common ancestor of the evicting and the
evicted cgroup as the limit pool, so that protection is computed for
the subtree the two share. For two cgroups that are both children of
the root, that ancestor is the root pool, and
dmem_cgroup_state_evict_valuable() returns true for it straight away:
the shortcut for a limit pool without a parent predates the
allocation-time protection series, when a limit pool could only come
from a cgroup that hit its dmem.max, which the root has none of.

The result is that dmem.min and dmem.low protect nothing between
siblings at the top level. Measured on an RX 9060 XT with three
cgroups under the root, dmem.min 4012 MiB each: A held 11 GiB, then B
and C allocated 4000 MiB each, one after the other. C's allocation
evicted 750 MiB of B, under its min, while A sat 5 GiB above its own.

Drop the shortcut. The ancestor walk below it already finds the root
as an ancestor of every pool, and dmem_cgroup_calculate_protection()
then gives the root's children their own min and low as effective
protection, which is what the same cgroups get one level down.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 kernel/cgroup/dmem.c | 3 ---
 1 file changed, 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel/cgroup/dmem.c b/kernel/cgroup/dmem.c
index 4683f3d68..0d38261a2 100644
--- a/kernel/cgroup/dmem.c
+++ b/kernel/cgroup/dmem.c
@@ -365,9 +365,6 @@ bool dmem_cgroup_state_evict_valuable(struct dmem_cgroup_pool_state *limit_pool,
 		return true;
 
 	if (limit_pool) {
-		if (!parent_dmemcs(limit_pool->cs))
-			return true;
-
 		for (pool = test_pool; pool && limit_pool != pool; pool = pool_parent(pool))
 			{}
 
